Create tests/test_morph_mcp.py for Python 3.9: a CONFORMANCE test for an MCP stdio server that does not exist yet. You are writing the specification's executable form; another author writes the server. Test it as a BLACK BOX: spawn it with subprocess as `python3 -m morph_mcp.server` (cwd = the project root, stdin/stdout pipes, text mode), speak the protocol over the pipes, and assert on the replies. Use unittest in the style of the test in the context. Standard library only.
Cover at least: (1) the initialize handshake returns protocolVersion and a serverInfo name, and advertises a tools capability; (2) tools/list returns the four tools deck_status, card_add, deck_submit, deck_collect, each with a description and an object inputSchema; (3) tools/call on deck_status returns a result whose content is a list with a text item; (4) an unknown method returns a JSON-RPC error with code -32601; (5) a notification (a message with no id) draws no response.
Always terminate the subprocess in a finally/cleanup and use a timeout on every read so a broken server fails the test instead of hanging it. Drive the server in a temporary directory (pass it as cwd or via an env var) so the test never writes into the real project backlog.
